## Hypothesised shape of the bug

The cascading symptom is consistent with one of two architectural
mistakes; without source access I cannot tell which is operative.

**Hypothesis A — slider value is a fraction of "current" duration,
re-read on every drag tick.**
During a drag, each mouse-moved event computes
`seekTime = sliderFraction * currentDuration` and dispatches a seek.
If the user drags past the right edge, the seek lands at or beyond
end-of-file, mpv emits `end-file`, IINA advances the playlist, and
`currentDuration` now refers to the *new* file. The drag is still
in progress, so the next mouse-moved event computes a new
`seekTime` against the new (likely shorter or different) duration,
again past the end, advancing again. Repeat → cascade.

**Hypothesis B — slider value is absolute seconds, but the scrub
handler does not stop dispatching across a file change.**
Each drag tick sends `seek <T> absolute`. The first one beyond
EOF triggers playlist advance. The next one, sent into the new
file, is still a large `T` that exceeds the new file's duration,
triggering another advance. Etc.

In both hypotheses the missing safeguard is the same: the scrub
gesture treats "what file am I scrubbing" as something that can
change mid-gesture, when it should be pinned for the duration of
the drag.

## Why it cascades rather than clamps (general)

Two things have to be true simultaneously:
- the seek-on-drag path does not clamp the requested time to
  `[0, currentDuration]` (or, equivalently, does not clamp the
  slider value to `[0, 1]` and then refuse to seek past EOF), AND
- the scrub gesture continues to issue seeks after the active file
  has changed under it, rather than terminating the gesture or
  ignoring further drag events until `mouseUp`.

If either guard were present, the cascade could not happen: the
first guard prevents the first over-the-edge seek from triggering
EOF at all (the player would just sit at the last frame); the
second guard prevents subsequent drag ticks from acting on a file
the user did not start scrubbing.

## Suggested fix (shape only)

Two complementary single-line changes, either of which alone would
likely stop the cascade:

1. In the scrub seek dispatcher, clamp the requested time to
   `max(0, min(requested, duration - epsilon))` so a drag past
   the right edge parks at the last frame instead of crossing EOF.
   This preserves the "scrubbing stops at end of file" half of the
   expected behaviour.
2. In the drag handler, capture the playing file's identity (path
   or playlist index) at `mouseDown`, and on each subsequent
   `mouseDragged` bail out early if the current file no longer
   matches. This prevents a still-active gesture from steering a
   different file. (The "advance to next file on `mouseUp` past
   the end" behaviour, if desired, can be implemented separately
   in the mouse-up handler.)

